@bio:

I am an Associate Professor in music theory at the School
of Music of the University of British Columbia, where I
teach courses on European 18th- and 19-century
compositional practice, techniques of graphic linear
analysis, empirical aesthetics, Francophone music, and
digital technology. My research has been presented at
interdisciplinary conferences in North America, Europe, and
the Middle East, and featured in <i>Music Perception</i>,
<i>Empirical Musicology Review</i>, <i>Auditory Perception &
Cognition</i>, and the <i>Journal of New Music Research</i>. Current
projects focus on empirical aesthetics, rhythmic polyphony,
and folk music of French-Canada, combining methods of close
study, computational musicology, ethnography, and
behavioral experimentation.

@@END: BIO